  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: S&EDisc 1
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "You\u2019re leading medical students on a lab tour where researchers
    are testing several potential drug candidates for future cancer studies. Even
    though preclinical studies haven\u2019t started yet, you explain that it\u2019s
    still important to follow the Food and Drug Administration\u2019s (FDA) good
    laboratory practices (GLP) in early lab work, such as tracking patient samples
    and keeping accurate records. You overhear one student tell another, \u201CThis
    is just early discovery work \u2014 the research team doesn\u2019t need to worry
    about meeting FDA standards yet.\u201D\n\nIs this true or false?"
  explanation: Even though the FDA does not directly review discovery-stage work,
    researchers still keep FDA guidelines in mind from the start. Good documentation,
    careful lab methods, and early attention to safety make it easier for the potential
    drug to move into preclinical animal studies and, eventually, human trials. Early
    planning helps protect future patients and strengthens the overall research process.
  correctAnswer: False
  incorrectAnswer1: True
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Discovery
